//? Title: LogitBoost with regression stumps
//? Subtitle: Actual Newton working responses, original diagonal fixture and finite arithmetic limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
LogitBoost builds an additive logistic predictor through weighted regression
fits. This report retains the original 300-candidate diagonal fixture and 40
rounds, exposes actual producing Newton states, and separates training
likelihood and accuracy from held-out performance or probability calibration.
)

\toc

> Producing Newton updates

For binary labels, p=1/(1+exp(-2F)), w=max(p*(1-p),1e-12), and z=clip((y-p)/w,
-max_response, max_response). Each round chooses the minimum weighted
squared-error regression stump in the actual finite candidate set and commits
F←F+0.5*f. Ties keep the earliest feature/threshold. Empty sides predict zero.
This is the implemented floored/clipped Newton procedure, not an unrestricted
exact Newton optimizer.

The tiny two-point example begins with weights0.25 and responses−2,+2. A
threshold between the points fits those responses exactly; its first committed
scores are−1,+1. The diagram and table use the real captured round.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/logitboost_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/logitboost.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::logitboost_trace({{-1}, {1}}, {0, 1}, 1);
    auto &r = t.rounds.front();
    std::print("| row | actual weight | actual response | committed probability |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, r.weights[i], r.responses[i],
                   r.probabilities[i]);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("inputs", {0, 0}, "Actual initial probabilities\n0.5, 0.5").node(
        "fit", {6, 0},
        std::format("Actual fitted stump\nleft {}, right {}",
                    r.stump.left_value, r.stump.right_value)).node(
        "output", {12, 0},
        std::format("Actual committed probabilities\n{:.4f}, {:.4f}",
                    r.probabilities[0], r.probabilities[1])).edge("inputs",
                                                                  "fit").edge(
        "fit", "output");
    d.save_svg("build/logitboost_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| row | actual weight | actual response | committed probability |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0.25 | -2 | 0.11920292202211755 |
| 1 | 0.25 | 2 | 0.8807970779778823 |
\image(figures/logitboost_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual first Newton fit on two points.)
\alttext(Captured weights and responses feed the actual selected stump and committed probabilities, without replaying training.)

> Separate held-out Iris application

A binary classification use on real data fits naturally here. The native Iris
dataset supplies versicolor and virginica, labelled0 and1, with Petal.Length and
Petal.Width as the only predictors. Every fifth row within each species is held
out before fitting:80 training rows and20 test rows. Forty rounds and the
default response cap are fixed in advance, with no test-based tuning. This
deterministic illustrative split is small and is not cross-validation. Its test
accuracy assesses classification on these particular unseen rows, not
calibration.

The native study helper owns the partition, fits only training rows and returns
actual held-out predictions. The annotated confusion matrix uses true-class rows
and predicted-class columns.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/logitboost_iris.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/logitboost_study.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/metrics.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto s = dm::algorithms::logitboost_iris_study();
    dm::nn::ConfusionMatrix cm(2);
    std::print("Actual training rows {}; actual held-out rows {}; fixed rounds {}.\n\n| held-out row | " "petal length | petal width | true class | actual prediction | actual probability |\n| ---: " "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n",
               s.training.labels.size(), s.testing.labels.size(),
               s.model.stumps.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.testing.labels.size(); ++i) {
        cm.add(s.testing.labels[i], s.predictions[i]);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i,
                   s.testing.features[i][0], s.testing.features[i][1],
                   s.testing.labels[i], s.predictions[i], s.probabilities[i]);
    }
    std::print("\nActual held-out accuracy {}.\n", cm.accuracy());
    cm.save_svg("build/logitboost_iris.svg",
                {.labels = {"versicolor", "virginica"}});
}
```
