> Complete round state and clipping controls

Each trace entry stores the actual clipped responses, floored weights, chosen
stump, committed probabilities and likelihood. It is emitted during the same fit
used by the ordinary API. The likelihood uses probabilities clipped
to[1e-12,1−1e-12] before logs, so it is explicitly a clipped training
diagnostic. The table compares independent clipping settings on the same tiny
input; it does not reconstruct an earlier trajectory.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/logitboost_clipping.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/logitboost.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| response cap | actual first left response | actual first right response | first committed " "positive probability |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (double cap : {.25, .5, 1., 2., 4.}) {
        auto t = dm::algorithms::logitboost_trace({{-1}, {1}}, {0, 1}, 1, cap);
        auto &r = t.rounds.front();
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", cap, r.responses[0],
                   r.responses[1], r.probabilities[1]);
        x.push_back(cap);
        y.push_back(r.probabilities[1]);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "first-round p", {37, 99, 235}, 6).x_label(
        "explicit response clipping cap").y_label("actual probability at x=1").title("Actual independent clipping controls").save_svg("build/logitboost_clipping.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| response cap | actual first left response | actual first right response | first committed positive probability |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.25 | -0.25 | 0.25 | 0.5621765008857981 |
| 0.5 | -0.5 | 0.5 | 0.6224593312018546 |
| 1 | -1 | 1 | 0.7310585786300049 |
| 2 | -2 | 2 | 0.8807970779778823 |
| 4 | -2 | 2 | 0.8807970779778823 |
\image(figures/logitboost_clipping.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual first-round response clipping controls.)
\alttext(Responses saturate at their unmodified magnitude2; smaller caps change the actual committed probability.)

> Finite thresholds and checked contracts

Complete training matrices must be finite, rectangular and have positive
dimension unless empty; labels must be binary and clipping positive finite even
for zero rounds. Original thresholds and finite midpoint arithmetic are retained
where valid. If a midpoint overflows, std::midpoint supplies a finite
alternative; if rounding reaches the upper knot, the lower knot preserves the
intended <= split. A finite threshold below the minimum is used when
representable. At the most negative finite value there is no smaller finite
threshold, so the equivalent constant prediction candidate uses the maximum knot
instead. Nonfinite weighted fits or accumulated scores throw rather than
silently returning a default stump.

Native tests verify analytic Newton values, actual chosen stumps and committed
probabilities, clipping, complete finite input guards, extreme thresholds,
capture admission, the original fixture and held-out Iris predictions. The
ten-report batch passed 101 native CTest cases, 34 directly instrumented
ASan/UBSan cases, 103 compiled/executed public examples, 515/515 scoped file
definitions plus 10 exact LCG class definitions, and ten representative clangd
hovers. Independent Python integer/Fraction, explicit-label graph search,
quadratic sequence DP and full weighted-stump references passed 69430 records
across all ten topics, including a separately fitted held-out Iris model. Frozen
ordinary compatibility passed 1000 Lex-BFS orders, 1000 library sorts, 100 LBG
codebooks, 100 complete five-round LogitBoost ensembles and 200 LLL bases;
corrected LBG final labels/objectives are intentionally excluded from historical
compatibility. All 5 complete programs executed and all 5 native figures were
rendered and visually reviewed, with clipped legends corrected and rerun.
HTML/Markdown computed-result and figure-byte checks gate legacy retirement.
Repeatable receipts are retained in `build/mepml-conversion/batch-204-213/`.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/logitboost_finite.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/logitboost.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    double m = std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
    auto t = dm::algorithms::logitboost_trace({{-m}, {m}}, {0, 1}, 1);
    auto &r = t.rounds.front();
    std::print("Actual extreme-input threshold {}; finite {}; predictions {}, {}.\n",
               r.stump.threshold, std::isfinite(r.stump.threshold),
               dm::algorithms::logitboost_predict(t.model, {-m}),
               dm::algorithms::logitboost_predict(t.model, {m}));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points({-1, 1}, r.probabilities, "actual probabilities", {37, 99, 235}, 6).x_limits(-1.5, 1.5).y_limits(0, 1.1).title("Actual full-range finite feature control").x_label("feature sign; actual magnitude is maximum finite double").y_label("first-round p").save_svg("build/logitboost_finite.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Actual extreme-input threshold 0; finite true; predictions 0, 1.
\image(figures/logitboost_finite.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual finite threshold on opposite maximum-magnitude inputs.)
\alttext(The displayed horizontal coordinates denote signs, explicitly avoiding an unrepresentable plotted range twice the maximum double.)

> References

J. Friedman, T. Hastie and R. Tibshirani(2000),
[Additive Logistic Regression: a Statistical View of Boosting|https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1016218223],
Annals of Statistics28(2),337–407. Its statistical motivation does not make
training accuracy into a validation estimate or establish calibration for this
particular clipped stump ensemble.
